,” “Mujer tropical” and “Recordando el pasado.” From Washington and, above all, from New York, Santos became one of the main promoters of merengue through the 1970s and into the 1980s, playing Madison Square Garden and Radio City Music Hall and opening the doors of rooms such as the Happy Hills Casino in upper Manhattan, the Cabo Rojeño and La Habana San Juan to a genre that Latin New York barely knew, at a time when salsa dominated the scene there. He shared stages with Tito Puente and Celia Cruz and worked alongside Santiago Cerón; according to the bandleader Chino Méndez, he was the first Dominican to front a big band in New York, playing mambo, guaracha, salsa, rumba and charanga against the era’s biggest names, among them Machito, Puente himself and Willie Rosario."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The orchestra","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Musicians who passed through Primitivo Santos y su Orquesta across the 1970s and 1980s included Héctor Zarzuela, Ite Jerez, Ray Martínez, "},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"occurrenceId":"0ec7868d-470e-40e4-adf9-a2921a58408c","artistId":"21a768a6-c0fd-4abf-88cf-b09cbc155c18","displayText":"Manolín González"}},{"type":"text","text":", Marino Solano and Juan Colón, along with the saxophonist "},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"occurrenceId":"dc5c7c7e-f3da-4c1e-8f2b-816b4251401a","artistId":"0dee2e87-7680-4300-8038-a57c419e76c3","displayText":"Crispín Fernández"}},{"type":"text","text":". Its 1980s lineup of singers included Carmen Luisa, Willie Contreras and Joa Aguilera."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Legacy","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In 2011 President Leonel Fernández decorated Santos, by decree, with the Orden del Mérito de Duarte, Sánchez y Mella — popularly known as the condecoración de los Padres de la Patria — and granted him a special pension, alongside other veteran musicians that included "},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"occurrenceId":"d1f17861-22cf-4cf5-8a1f-e1b2617aaf11","artistId":"f625be23-cfa4-43fe-8bb5-0879b2fa492f","displayText":"Vinicio Franco"}},{"type":"text","text":". It was, in effect, the only formal recognition his home country ever gave him: as recently as October 2025, his own former lead singer, Camboy Estévez, publicly pointed out that Santos had died without ever winning a Casandra or a Soberano, grouping him with two other historic figures similarly passed over, Aníbal de Peña and Luis Kalaff. Santos had already been retired for years when he died in Miami in 2018, at eighty-three."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'published', id, 1 FROM artists WHERE slug = 'primitivo-santos-y-su-orquesta'
